.TH IOPROC 2
.SH NAME
ioproc,
iocall,
ioread,
iowrite,
ioread_full \- slave I/O processes for threaded programs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libthread"
.PP
.nf
.B "ioproc :: proc() -> ^Ioproc"
.B "ioread :: proc(io: ^Ioproc, fd: int, buf: []u8) -> i64"
.B "iowrite :: proc(io: ^Ioproc, fd: int, data: []u8) -> i64"
.B "ioread_full :: proc(io: ^Ioproc, fd: int, buf: []u8) -> bool"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A thread that calls
.I read
or
.I write
directly
.RI ( "see " read (2))
blocks its whole proc, and every other thread of that proc with it.
An
.I ioproc
is a proc whose only job is to make one such call at a time on a
thread's behalf: the calling
.I thread
parks on a channel while the call is made, and the proc it belongs to
keeps running its other threads.
.PP
.I Ioproc
makes a new proc and returns a pointer to the
.B Ioproc
that represents it.
It calls
.I exits
through
.I proccreate
(see
.IR wait (2)
and
.IR thread (2))
rather than return an error when there is no memory for it.
.PP
.I Ioread
and
.I iowrite
are
.I read
and
.I write
.RI ( "see " read (2))
made in the proc
.I io
belongs to.
The result is the kernel's: a count, or a negative
.IR errno .
.I Ioread_full
calls
.I ioread
until
.I buf
is full or a call answers zero or less, and answers whether it filled
the buffer.
.PP
One
.I ioproc
makes one call at a time.
Two threads that share one take turns.
A program gives each thing that can block a proc of its own: a server
typically has one
.I ioproc
for the pipe it serves and one for the device it reads.
.PP
.I Iocall
is the primitive
.I ioread
and
.I iowrite
are built from.
It sends a call record to
.IR io 's
proc over a channel, and waits on another for the reply.
The record lives on the calling thread's stack, which is safe because
the thread is parked until the reply names it.
.SH EXAMPLES
A key thread reads a device through an
.I ioproc
so the rest of its program's threads keep running:
.IP
.EX
io := libthread.ioproc()
for {
	n := libthread.ioread(io, fd, buf[:])
	if n <= 0 {
		break
	}
	handle(buf[:n])
}
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libthread/io.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR thread (2),
.IR read (2)
.SH BUGS
There is no
.I iointerrupt
or
.I ioflush
to cut a call short from outside: an
.I ioproc
parked in a device read stays parked until the read returns.
There is no
.I iodial
or
.I iosleep .
